  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhanced institutional and technical capacity of tripartite constituents to drive a transformative agenda on gender equality, non-discrimination and inclusion, and to expand decent work opportunities</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Decent work is the ILO&apos;s primary goal in Indonesia, with a strong focus on ensuring equal opportunities for women, minority groups and people in vulnerable situations. The ILO works to promote equality of opportunity and treatment in employment, reduce stigma and discrimination, and address structural barriers that prevent these groups from accessing decent work and sustainable livelihoods.
The ILO supports efforts to overcome the challenges faced by vulnerable workers, including persons with disabilities, migrant women workers, minorities such as refugees, people living with HIV, and other disadvantaged groups. Particular emphasis is placed on advancing decent work in and through the care economy, including supporting the Government in developing and strengthening policies related to care work and care services. To achieve these objectives, the Office is currently undertaking &#x2014; and will continue to undertake &#x2014; actions including: Promoting gender equality and a human rights-based approach, particularly for persons with disabilities, women and girls, and strengthening social protection systems. This includes advocating for increased social investment in care services, especially childcare, to help address the disproportionate burden of unpaid care and domestic work carried by women, through research, knowledge sharing, technical advice and capacity building. The ILO will also facilitate partnerships and labour market linkages, by connecting private sector firms, social partners and training institutions to expand access, retention and career advancement opportunities for women workers, persons with disabilities and other communities in vulnerable situations.</narrative>
  </description>
